    historyContent: {
      origins: "Believed to have evolved from the Spanish game 'ChinchÃ³n' and the broader European 'Continental Rummy' family in the mid-20th century. The name 'Loba' evokes the 'wolfish' nature of stealing opponents' discards and the predatory approach to collecting cards.",
      originCountries: [
        'Argentina',
        'Bolivia',
        'Uruguay',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1940s: Rummy variants spread through the Rio de la Plata region of South America.',
        "1960s: 'Loba' becomes a favorite social game for clubs and families in Buenos Aires and Montevideo.",
        '1980s: Regional variations solidify; Loba de Mar (Sea Wolf) variant popularized in coastal regions.',
        'Present: One of the most common card games in Argentine and Uruguayan household culture, often played at summer resorts and family gatherings.',
        '2010s: Digital versions gain popularity on mobile platforms across Latin America.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game's scoring system, where high-value cards like Jokers carry massive penalties, was designed to discourage 'holding' high cards to the end of a round. The multi-round structure with escalating contracts adds strategic depth.",
      cultural: "Commonly played as 'Loba de Mar' (Sea Wolf) in coastal regions of Argentina and Uruguay. The game is a staple of South American social card gaming, bridging generations at family gatherings.",
    },
